  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 243 AM CDT Tue Aug 18 2026\n\nWednesday looks to bring a large range in afternoon highs, with\nareas north of Highway 60 remaining between 85-95 degrees, and\nareas south of the highway climbing between 95-105. If this\noccurs, then heat index values would once again climb between \n100-110 degrees, and the heat advisory would need to be extended\nthrough Wednesday. There's still much uncertainty in how the \novernight convection will influence temperatures, so there's low\nconfidence in those higher temps being fully realized. Because \nof that, decided to hold off on extending the Advisory into \nWednesday for now, but can't rule out a future extension in \nlater packages.\n\nThe aforementioned shortwave trough will move out of the Plains\nand towards the Mid Mississippi/Ohio Valleys during the day on\nWednesday. Periodic showers/storms are possible (20-30%)\nthroughout the day, however the better chances (50-70%) for \nstorms exist in the evening and overnight. The Storm Prediction\nCenter has highlighted the southeast portion of the CWA in a \nSlight risk (2 of 5), as this area will see the greater \ninstability (1000-2000 J/kg MUCAPE) and shear (0-6kt bulk shear \nof 40-50kts). The primary hazard with these storms will once \nagain be damaging winds, however if key mesoscale features can \nline up, all modes will be possible, meaning a lower-end tornado\nand hail threat.",
